    {
      "id": "b9e994d7-5853-44fa-a0ea-da949d8b56b2",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -5616,
        832
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 480,
        "height": 976,
        "content": "## Weather-Triggered Delivery Hold & Incident Automation\n\n### How it works\n\nThis workflow polls weather data for a configured delivery region and evaluates whether conditions are severe enough to place deliveries on hold. When severe weather is detected, it checks for duplicate active alerts, logs a new alert, retrieves delivery manifests, and determines whether any deliveries are impacted. If deliveries are affected, an AI agent drafts structured incident details, creates a ServiceNow incident, prepares affected-store notifications, and updates operations through Slack while logging completion; a separate error path records failures and alerts Ops.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n- Configure the schedule trigger frequency for how often weather should be checked.\n- Update the region settings node with the target region name, ZIP code, latitude, longitude, and any cooldown/severity thresholds used downstream.\n- Set up credentials for Open-Meteo HTTP access if required by your environment, PostgreSQL, Slack, ServiceNow, and Google Gemini.\n- Ensure the PostgreSQL tables for alert logs, delivery manifests, no-impact records, completion updates, and error logs exist with the fields referenced by the workflow.\n- Configure Slack channels/users for operational summaries and failure alerts, and confirm ServiceNow incident fields match the AI-generated structured output.\n\n### Customization\n\nAdjust the severe-weather criteria, cooldown window, delivery-manifest query, AI incident prompt/output schema, Slack message formats, and ServiceNow assignment/routing rules to match local operations."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
